<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Continuum — entitlements for the sideloaded build.

  Distribution is sideloading with custom signing, so these are simply present rather than
  requested: App Store review is out of scope.

  HOW JIT ACTUALLY WORKS ON iOS, because two earlier versions of this comment were wrong in
  opposite directions. It is not unconditional, and it is not impossible either.

  The two `com.apple.security.cs.*` keys below are macOS HARDENED RUNTIME keys. On iOS they are
  not the switch for executable memory, and they are ignored. That part of the correction stands.

  What iOS actually requires is `get-task-allow`, and then an external debugger. A process that is
  being debugged gets `CS_DEBUGGED`, and the kernel permits executable memory for it. That is the
  mechanism every JIT-using sideloaded app on a non-jailbroken iPhone relies on, through StikDebug
  or StikJIT over a local VPN loopback, with no computer needed after the first pairing.

  `get-task-allow` IS grantable, but only by a DEVELOPMENT provisioning profile. Apple does not
  whitelist it on distribution profiles. So the signing choice decides whether a recompiler is
  possible, and signing this app with a distribution identity silently rules it out.

  `dynamic-codesigning` is deliberately NOT here. That is the TrollStore/jailbreak route, it
  cannot be carried by any profile, and a restricted entitlement the signing identity cannot
  satisfy can make an install fail outright. The debugger route needs nothing of the sort.

  Sources: StikJIT's own integration guide states the process receiving JIT must have
  `get-task-allow`, and Apple's developer forums state that entitlement is whitelisted only by
  development profiles.

  The two memory entitlements do different jobs and both are needed:

    increased-memory-limit        raises the jetsam cap — resident, dirty pages. This is
                                 what stops the app being killed for the memory it
                                 actually touches.
    extended-virtual-addressing  raises the virtual address space beyond 4 GB. This is
                                 what allows the large sparse reservation every dynarec
                                 wants for fast-mem: a PROT_NONE reservation has no
                                 resident pages, so it costs address space and nothing
                                 else, and does not affect jetsam risk at all.

  Conflating them is the easy mistake. Without the first, a Switch title is killed for
  using 6 GB. Without the second, the reservation fails before it starts.
-->
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- THE ONE THAT MATTERS ON iOS. Lets a debugger attach, which is what makes executable
	     memory available: StikDebug and StikJIT both require it of the process receiving JIT.

	     ONLY A DEVELOPMENT PROVISIONING PROFILE CAN CARRY THIS. Signing with a distribution
	     identity drops it, and a dropped `get-task-allow` means no recompiler, which means no
	     N64, PSP, 3DS or Switch however good the graphics path gets. The app reports whether
	     its own installed copy has it, so this is checkable rather than assumed; see
	     `jit_probe.rs` and the JIT line in the diagnostics. -->
	<key>get-task-allow</key>
	<true/>

	<!-- JIT. mmap with MAP_JIT, then pthread_jit_write_protect_np per thread, then
	     sys_icache_invalidate — which is NOT optional on arm64: omitting it works in the
	     simulator and crashes on device, intermittently.

	     A macOS hardened-runtime key, kept because it is harmless and correct for a future
	     macOS target, but it is NOT what enables this on iOS. See the header. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
	<true/>

	<!-- Some recompilers map a page W and X simultaneously rather than toggling. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
	<true/>

	<key>com.apple.developer.kernel.increased-memory-limit</key>
	<true/>

	<key>com.apple.developer.kernel.extended-virtual-addressing</key>
	<true/>

	<!-- Debug builds get a further raise, which is worth having while measuring a Switch
	     title's real footprint before deciding what to gate on. -->
	<key>com.apple.developer.kernel.increased-debugging-memory-limit</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
